---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import CodeCard from "../components/CodeCard.astro";
import InstallTabs from "../components/InstallTabs.astro";
import site from "../data/site.ts";
import siteData from "../data/site-data.json";

const title = "Install sf-intelligence for Claude, Cursor, VS Code & Codex";
const description =
  "Try the demo org in two minutes, then connect your own Salesforce org: read it once with init and refresh, add one snippet to your AI assistant, and ask.";
const dateModified = "2026-10-10";

// HowTo rich results were retired by Google (2023), so the page is modelled as
// a TechArticle, which Bing and answer engines still parse.
const jsonld = [
  {
    "@type": "TechArticle",
    headline: "Install sf-intelligence for a Salesforce org",
    description:
      "Try the built-in demo org, then connect your own Salesforce org to sf-intelligence, a free, read-only Salesforce MCP server, in Claude Code, Claude Desktop, Cursor, VS Code with Copilot, or Codex.",
    author: { "@id": `${site.url}/#author` },
    publisher: { "@id": `${site.url}/#org` },
    dateModified,
    mainEntityOfPage: `${site.url}/getting-started`,
    proficiencyLevel: "Beginner",
    dependencies: "Node.js 20+; for your own org, the Salesforce CLI (sf) logged in to it",
  },
];

const firstRun = `npx -y sf-intelligence init --target-org my-org
npx -y sf-intelligence refresh`;

const fullProfile = `"env": { "SFI_TOOL_PROFILE": "full" }`;
---

<Base
  title={title}
  description={description}
  path="/getting-started"
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: "Getting started", path: "/getting-started" }]}
  jsonld={jsonld}
  dateModified={dateModified}
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href="/">Home</a><span class="sep" aria-hidden="true">/</span><a href="/getting-started">Getting started</a>
      </nav>
      <h1>Get started in three steps</h1>
      <p class="lede">Try the demo org, connect your own org, then ask. You need <a href="https://nodejs.org" rel="noopener">Node.js</a> 20 or later. Connecting your own org also needs the Salesforce CLI.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" style="padding-top:56px">
    <div class="wrap">
      <ol class="path-steps">
        <!-- Step 1 -->
        <li class="path-step" id="demo">
          <h2>Try the demo org</h2>
          <div class="step-body">
            <p>The demo org is built in: a made-up solar-installation company called Verdant. There is nothing to log in to, and it takes about two minutes. Pick your assistant, add the snippet, and restart the assistant.</p>
            <InstallTabs mode="demo" idPrefix="gs-demo" />
            <p>Then ask, for example: <strong>“Can I delete the Amount field on Invoice?”</strong>, <strong>“Who can edit Invoices?”</strong> or <strong>“What runs when a Project is saved?”</strong> You can <a href="/demo">see the answers first</a>.</p>
          </div>
        </li>

        <!-- Step 2 -->
        <li class="path-step" id="connect">
          <h2>Connect your own org</h2>
          <div class="step-body">
            <h3 id="prerequisites">Before you start</h3>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>Node.js 20 or later.</strong> Check with <code>node --version</code>.</li>
              <li><strong>The Salesforce CLI, logged in to your org.</strong> Run <code>sf org login web --alias my-org</code> if you haven’t yet.</li>
              <li><strong>A folder to work in.</strong> Run it from your Salesforce project folder, or any empty folder: <code>init</code> creates <code>sfdx-project.json</code> if there isn’t one.</li>
            </ul>

            <h3 id="first-run">Read your org once</h3>
            <p>From that folder, run these two commands. sf-intelligence reads your org’s metadata, read-only, into an <code>org-kb</code> folder next to your project. The first read takes a few minutes on a typical sandbox. Run <code>refresh</code> again whenever your org changes.</p>
            <CodeCard id="gs-first-run" code={firstRun} help="Use the alias you logged in with. It never deploys or changes anything in your org." />

            <h3 id="install">Add it to your assistant</h3>
            <p>Pick your assistant and copy the snippet. Every snippet points at your <code>org-kb</code> folder with <code>--vault</code>; use the full path, because most assistants don’t start the server inside your project.</p>
            <InstallTabs mode="org" idPrefix="gs-org" />
          </div>
        </li>

        <!-- Step 3 -->
        <li class="path-step" id="ask">
          <h2>Ask</h2>
          <div class="step-body">
            <p>Ask in plain words, the way you would ask a colleague. For example:</p>
            <ul>
              <li>Can I delete <em>this field</em>?</li>
              <li>Who can edit <em>this object</em>?</li>
              <li>What runs when <em>an Opportunity</em> is saved?</li>
              <li>What sets <em>Stage</em> to <em>Closed Lost</em>?</li>
              <li>Is this deployment risky? <em>(paste a package.xml or a git diff file list)</em></li>
              <li>Brief me on <em>the Case object</em>.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>If you connect the assistant before the first <code>refresh</code> finishes, the server starts in setup mode and tells your assistant what to run next. Ask it “what do you need from me?”</p>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section band" aria-labelledby="details-title">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="kicker">For developers</span>
        <h2 id="details-title">The details</h2>
        <p>Checking the connection, tool profiles, keeping the snapshot fresh, and fixing common problems.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <h3 id="verify">Check that it’s connected</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Claude Code:</strong> run <code>/mcp</code>. The server should be listed as connected.</li>
          <li><strong>VS Code:</strong> run <strong>MCP: List Servers</strong> from the command palette.</li>
          <li><strong>Codex:</strong> run <code>codex mcp list</code>.</li>
          <li><strong>Any assistant:</strong> ask it to run <code>sfi.health_check</code>. A <code>healthy</code> result means everything is wired up.</li>
        </ul>

        <p>Step-by-step guides for two hosts with their own quirks: <a href="/setup/vs-code-copilot">VS Code + GitHub Copilot</a> and <a href="/setup/cursor">Cursor</a>.</p>

        <h3 id="tool-profile">Tool profile</h3>
        <p>By default your assistant sees a small core set of tools, which keeps its tool list short. Every other analysis is still reachable through <code>sfi.run_analysis</code>. To list all {siteData.listedToolCount} tools directly, add this to the server’s config:</p>
        <CodeCard id="gs-full-profile" file="Server config" code={fullProfile} />
        <p>See the <a href="/configuration">configuration reference</a> for every option.</p>

        <h3 id="shorter-command">A shorter command</h3>
        <p><code>npx -y sf-intelligence …</code> works without installing anything. Install it globally to get a short <code>sfi</code> command instead:</p>
        <CodeCard id="gs-global" code="npm install -g sf-intelligence" />

        <h3 id="fresh">Keeping the snapshot fresh</h3>
        <p>Answers come from the last <code>refresh</code>. Every answer is stamped with when that was, and <code>sfi.health_check</code> flags a snapshot older than a week. When your org changes, run <code>npx -y sf-intelligence refresh</code> again.</p>

        <h3 id="troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</h3>
        <table class="doc-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Likely cause</th><th>Fix</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>VS Code shows no server and no error</td><td>The config uses <code>mcpServers</code></td><td>VS Code reads <code>servers</code>. Use the VS Code snippet above.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Only a setup tool appears</td><td>The server can’t find your <code>org-kb</code> folder: no <code>--vault</code>, a relative path, or no <code>refresh</code> yet</td><td>Run <code>refresh</code>, then pass the full path to <code>org-kb</code>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Cursor doesn’t show the tools</td><td>Cursor didn’t reload its MCP config</td><td>Turn the server off and on in Settings → MCP, or restart Cursor.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Codex times out on first start</td><td>The first download is slow</td><td>Set <code>startup_timeout_sec = 30</code> in <code>~/.codex/config.toml</code>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>refresh</code> fails to retrieve</td><td>The Salesforce CLI isn’t logged in, or the alias is wrong</td><td><code>sf org login web --alias my-org</code>, then run <code>refresh</code> again.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>For anything else, run <code>npx -y sf-intelligence doctor</code>. It checks the Salesforce CLI, the snapshot, your login and the graph, and prints a fix for each problem.</p>

        <h3 id="plugin">Claude Code plugin</h3>
        <p>Installing sf-intelligence as a Claude Code plugin adds {siteData.skillCount} skills that switch on when you talk about Salesforce, and {siteData.commandCount} slash commands such as <code>/sfi-onboard</code>, a guided first run. The plugin registers the same server, so you don’t need a separate <code>claude mcp add</code>.</p>
        <CodeCard id="gs-plugin" file="Inside Claude Code" code={"/plugin marketplace add PranavNagrecha/Salesforce-Intelligence\n/plugin install sf-intelligence@sf-intelligence"} />

        <h3 id="more">Live data, multiple orgs and other options</h3>
        <p>Answers come from the snapshot by default. To turn on the opt-in, read-only live lookups (record counts, samples, org limits), or to work with several orgs, see the <a href="/configuration">configuration reference</a>.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
